        "abstract": "The contamination of spacesuits by lunar regolith dust during extravehicular activity (EVA) is one of the biggest problems facing NASA's planned return to the moon with the Artemis Program. To control this, we propose using cryogenic CO<inf xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">2</inf> spray as a practical and efficient method of mitigating dust contamination. This form of cryogenic cleaning is a novel application of technology widely utilized for removing metallic dust particles, like those found in lunar environments, from microelectronics, photomasks, and other sensitive surfaces. The proposed solution includes a handheld CO<inf xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">2</inf> sprayer with an adjustable nozzle for alternation between general and localized cleaning, as well as a potential large-scale design for full-suit cleaning.",
